  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA deep area of subsidence sits across the Mid Atlantic region and \nstretches into the Lower Great Lakes. One trough was able to ride \nalong its northwest side and into Canada, but our local area will \ncontinue to be dry today through at least Saturday as the \nsurface high pressure area combines with the mid level ridging \nto provide dry air. With the dry air, today, it will be possible\nto generate low level lapse rates quick enough to produce gusty\nwinds to 20 to 30 mph, especially if mixing takes hold. \nHowever, there are some models that have some low level moisture\nthat intrudes on these rates. Therefore, the gusts are \npossible, but come with lower confidence.\n\nNow, by the time we get to Friday night, the surface high pressure \narea and mid level ridging are being impinged on by the large mid \nlevel trough pushing through the Western US. A fairly vigorous \nshortwave is able to dig down into Texas and make its way towards \nthe area Sunday. The issue is that it's dampening out by the time it \ngets here and it's pretty quick through the area. A delayed arrival \ncloser to the GFS depiction could mean more instability gets to the \narea, but the thin line of moisture could prevent any local impacts \nfrom rain and potential storms even if it has 1 to 2 SDs above \nnormal PWATs. \n\nSurface high pressure takes over for Monday and this allows for a \nreturn to dry air. Cold air also pushes into the area, and \nwhile our lows were in the 30s and 40s this morning, they'll be\nable to drop down into the mid to upper 20s Monday night. We \nrecently had this magnitude of cold air to start the day between\n11/14 and 11/17. These cold morning temps are expected to stay \nwith us through the end of next week. This is as our highs \nflatten out around 40 degrees next week after Monday. One \ninteresting aspect about Monday and Tuesday is that with the \ncold air filtering in, 850 mb temps cool below zero bottoming \nout around -9 or -10C Monday night. This produces a Delta T \naround -15 to -20C so this is probably more of a lake effect \ncloud scenario than a widespread lake effect rain event.\n\nBeyond this point, different model handling of a wave moving through \nthe Central Plains provides less confidence on main forcing between \nwhether the main wave is the southern along the Gulf States \nwithin the subtropical jet or within the Pacific Jet across the \nNorthern Tier of the US.",
